1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,592
Customs will reject a declaration long
before a crane moves. Your API should do
the same: a request body that parses as
JSON can still be garbage, and that
garbage must die at the edge.

2
00:00:11,711 --> 00:00:20,903
Without bean validation, customs
controllers grow hand-written checks —
null tests, HS-code length tests, nested
if blocks that return ad-hoc error maps.

3
00:00:21,024 --> 00:00:30,240
Rules drift between "create declaration"
and "amend declaration." Services re-check
the same fields because they do not trust
the controller.

4
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:38,903
Spring’s integration with Jakarta Bean
Validation gives you declarative
constraints on DTOs and a standard failure
path when those constraints fail.

5
00:00:39,024 --> 00:00:51,335
Put the rules on the request type the
controller accepts: Look at this java for
a moment. Look at this java for a moment.
@Valid is the switch that turns
annotations into enforcement.

6
00:00:51,456 --> 00:01:05,496
Without it, Spring binds the JSON and your
method runs even when hsCode is blank.
With it, the HandlerAdapter validates
before your method body executes. Failure
throws MethodArgumentNotValidException.

7
00:01:05,615 --> 00:01:17,376
That exception is your signal — later
lessons turn it into Problem+JSON; today,
know that the edge rejected the payload
before CustomsDesk saw it. Nested objects
and collections need care.

8
00:01:17,495 --> 00:01:30,000
Annotate nested beans with @Valid so
constraints cascade. Use @NotEmpty on
lists that must contain at least one
content line. Group constraints when
create and update rules differ.

9
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:41,735
For query parameters and path variables,
@Validated on the controller plus
constraint annotations on method
parameters covers the non-body cases. Look
at this java for a moment.

10
00:01:41,855 --> 00:01:50,063
Validation is structural honesty, not
business policy. "HS code format looks
right" belongs on the DTO.

11
00:01:50,183 --> 00:02:02,927
"This parcel is already cleared and cannot
be redeclared" belongs in the domain
service and should surface as a conflict,
not as a bean-validation failure. Mixing
those layers produces error messages
nobody can route.

12
00:02:03,048 --> 00:02:15,096
People forget @Valid and then blame Bean
Validation for "not working." Others put
@NotNull on entity fields and expect every
repository write to be protected —
entities are not the API boundary.

13
00:02:15,216 --> 00:02:27,912
A third mistake is returning raw binding
errors as a pile of field names with no
stable envelope, so mobile customs apps
cannot render a single error screen.
Malformed declarations now die at the
controller.

14
00:02:28,032 --> 00:02:34,776
Controllers and services still throw other
failures — unknown parcel ids, conflicts,
unexpected bugs.

15
00:02:34,896 --> 00:02:46,488
The open craft is turning those exceptions
into one coherent HTTP error model instead
of a container HTML error page for a JSON
client. That craft is exception handling.
